  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "The memory guard's test takes the quarter gigabyte it writes from the system, with freed pages left in the C allocator's heap first, so no allocator can hand it pages the process holds.\nClouds and the fog drift with the wind, each camera's clouds keep the noise of their own seed, and a scripted run of the feature test puts the tester's settings back however it ends.\nThe pictures read for the items of the four days before this one are kept among the readings, one or two an item, each with its caption.\nThe Slang scratch folder is the user's own, and slangc is looked for only in the checkout a program stands in.\nA text set to what it says is left unchanged, and a stream of samples that runs dry comes back on its first channel.\nA shader instance's values are set where it stands, instances and ray scenes can be taken away, a buffer nothing holds is forgotten, and a text that grows while it is read is asked for again.\nPreStartup systems are ordered as they ask, and a behavior method spread across threads reaches no world on the thread that called it either.\nThe game of life's capture stops at sixty steps and shows the image the last of them wrote, so a runner ready a frame sooner draws the same picture.\nAn app gives back the memory its GPU driver freed as it ends, and the renderer's test hosts are held to a cap fitted to the runner, a part near its cap said on the page.\nThe renderer job runs ten parts laid out by what each class or case took on the runner, five pairs in a pack, and keeps each part's results for laying them out again.\nA script reload whose migration throws is taken back whole, every component's value migrated before any is moved.\nThe test script takes a bridge test binary that crashed on Windows as lost, and the guide says a fixed step reads its messages through a stage that runs once a frame and which way round a C# matrix multiplies in a shader.\nA clip that ends as its model is despawned is said without its name, the overlay times frames on the machine's clock, and a stop kills no process that took a left-over session's id.\nThe tests that need a renderer run on every push on Linux's render bridge under lavapipe, in parts a file lays out by their minutes there, and the cases that would take hours there skip themselves on a device that draws on the processor.\nThe session file is its owner's alone from the moment it is made, a panic that ends the run is written to the crash file and thrown, and a canceled streamed read never lands after its cancel.\nA ray scene's test lays its slab after the stand-ins on a device that draws on the processor, the thunder test runs on a set frame time, and the capture test asks for its picture once its pipelines are ready.\nTwo touching bodies bounce as the bouncier of the two does, and the spatial scale is documented as what a world unit is multiplied by to give meters.\nAn export builds its bridge again when any file the bridge is built from is newer than it, and three tests fail on the code they cover when that code is broken.\nA component's remove and clone hooks reach every handle its layout holds, and two behaviors of one name declared in different types both build.\nA query takes a buffer of its own for each walk, a behavior method's copy of a component is written back only where the method changed it, and pausing or resuming a sound keeps its volume.\nAsset fields read from scenes, saves and overrides share one handle for each asset, and a load releases the shared handles no field holds once it has been read.\nAn inspector edit spread over the selection undoes on every entity it reached, a loaded scene starts with nothing to undo, and a redone duplicate takes the edits made on its copies.\nA load leaves a scene the game loaded itself standing, a ulong past a long's reach is saved and read back, and a scene a save names that cannot be read is said with its entries kept.\nThe fog's froxels move on once a frame under a weather, a storm strikes as its rate climbs, a machine on an entity with no animated scene stops, each placing of a subscene saves apart, a cleared reference saves as none, and the inspector's undo keeps entities apart.\nSwarm's play holds the game's clock between its commands, so it reads the swarm alike on a graphics card and on the software device.",
